# Solutions

**1.** Γ[φ_cl]=W[J]−∫J φ_cl, with φ_cl=δW/δJ.

**2.** δΓ/δφ_cl= (δW/δJ)(δJ/δφ_cl)−J−φ_cl(δJ/δφ_cl)=φ_cl(δJ/δφ_cl)−J−φ_cl(δJ/δφ_cl)=−J.

**3.** Worked example 1.

**4.** A connected diagram that remains connected after cutting any one internal line.

**5.** φ_cl already includes the full propagator acting on J; subtracting ∫Jφ_cl and changing variables to φ_cl removes those lines from the vertices.

**6.** Γ'' W''=−1, and W'' is the connected two-point function S_F′.

**7.** Γ''(p̸=m) has a zero (a pole of S_F′ at the physical mass).

**8.** δΓ/δφ_cl=0.

**9.** Yes. Gauge invariance is a symmetry of Γ for gauge-invariant operators, equivalent to Lesson 71.

**10.** A tadpole is a 1PI one-point function. δΓ/δφ=0 says the 1PI one-point vertex vanishes in the true vacuum, so tadpoles are already absorbed into the definition of φ_cl.

